# ============================================================
# Skill-based architecture — environment variables (template)
# ============================================================
# Copy to .env and edit. .env is gitignored; this template is committed.
# Loaded automatically by main.py via python-dotenv.

# --- LLM model selection ---
# Any LiteLLM-supported model works. The skill-based architecture's
# design point is that deterministic Python skills absorb the per-step
# reasoning load, so a smaller open-weights planner is usually enough.
# Pick ONE block below (uncomment exactly one LLM_MODEL line).

# --- Option A: Anthropic Claude (frontier; strong baseline) ---
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-opus-4-7
# Or a smaller Anthropic model:
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# --- Option B: OpenAI-compatible endpoint (vLLM / Ollama / LocalAI / etc.) ---
# Set OPENAI_API_BASE to your endpoint (e.g. local vLLM, on-prem GPU box,
# SSH-tunnelled remote). The literal string "none" is accepted as the
# API key by most OpenAI-compatible servers that do not enforce auth.
# OPENAI_API_BASE=http://<your-vllm-host>:<port>/v1
# OPENAI_API_KEY=none
# LLM_MODEL=openai/<model-id-as-served-by-your-endpoint>
#
# Example: a Qwen 3.6 27B AWQ deployment served by vLLM
# LLM_MODEL=openai/cyankiwi/Qwen3.6-27B-AWQ-INT4
#
# Note for vLLM users: if your model emits Hermes-style <tool_call> XML
# inside message.content (i.e. tool_calls field stays empty), start vLLM
# with `--enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser hermes`. Without
# those flags, llm_client.py falls back to client-side XML parsing.

# --- Option C: OpenAI directly ---
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# LLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o

# --- Per-role override (optional) ---
# Falls back to LLM_MODEL if unset. Useful when you want a different
# model for the planner than for any future verifier or sub-component.
# PLANNER_MODEL=
